1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,671
Book Three, Proposition Thirty-Four.

2
00:00:02,671 --> 00:00:08,674
From a given circle, to cut off a segment admitting an angle equal to a given rectilineal angle.

3
00:00:08,674 --> 00:00:13,677
Let A B C be the given circle and the angle at D be the given rectilineal angle.

4
00:00:13,677 --> 00:00:18,930
It is required to cut off from circle A B C a segment admitting an angle equal to D.

5
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:23,994
Draw the straight line E F touching the circle A B C at the point B.

6
00:00:23,994 --> 00:00:34,920
On the line F B, and at the point B on it, construct the angle F B C equal to the given angle D by One Twenty-Three, with the chord B C cutting the circle at C.

7
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:50,938
Because the straight line E F touches the circle A B C, and the chord B C is drawn across from the point of contact at B, by Three Thirty-Two the angle F B C equals the angle B A C constructed in the alternate segment B A C.

8
00:00:50,938 --> 00:01:01,175
But the angle F B C was constructed equal to the angle at D; therefore, by Common Notion One, the angle in the segment B A C equals the angle at D.

9
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:11,620
Thus from the given circle A B C, the segment B A C has been cut off admitting an angle equal to the given rectilineal angle D, as was required to do.

10
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:23,514
Proposition Three Thirty-Four is the dual of Three Thirty-Three: instead of constructing a circle on a given chord, we slice a given circle with a chord at a prescribed angle to its tangent.

11
00:01:23,514 --> 00:01:37,524
As Heath notes, one could alternatively construct a central angle double the given angle D, or simply draw a diameter when D is right; yet Euclid's tangent-chord construction requires only copying the single angle D at the boundary.

12
00:01:37,524 --> 00:01:52,320
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred forty-two combines One Twenty-Three and Three Thirty-Two into a surgical tool used immediately in Book Four, Proposition Two, to inscribe a triangle equiangular with any given triangle inside a circle.

13
